  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\int (4-{cos}^{2}\u20614x+{sin}^{2}\u20614x)\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta ={cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta -{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20618x={cos}^{2}\u20614x-{sin}^{2}\u20614x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-{cos}^{2}\u20614x+{sin}^{2}\u20614x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-[{cos}^{2}\u20614x-{sin}^{2}\u20614x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4-\\operatorname{cos}\u20618x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}\u20618x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
